# Golden files are byte-compared in tests — never let git line-ending
# conversion touch them (emitters write LF; CRLF checkout would fail the
# byte-identity contract, plan.md §3.2).
test/golden/** -text

# The frozen catalog fixture is byte-compared with LF-only production output -
# preserve its exact bytes across Windows checkouts.
test/fixtures/inspiration/catalog-v1.json -text

# The UAT PowerShell was partitioned VERBATIM out of the three fences in
# documentation/inspiration-real-artwork-uat.md and is re-verified line-by-line.
# Checkout line-ending conversion would break that byte-identity, and the
# UTF-8 BOM these files carry is load-bearing (PS 5.1 decodes a BOM-less .ps1
# as ANSI/cp1252 and can misparse a non-ASCII character with no parse error).
scripts/uat/*.ps1 -text

# Phase I2I.2 Step 36 real-artwork demo. test/inspiration.demo.test.ts byte-
# compares this whole tree against a live production `from-image` regeneration,
# so checkout line-ending conversion would break that byte-identity exactly the
# way it would for a golden file. The selected raster is the operator-verified
# M3a byte sequence and is marked binary explicitly - it must never be sniffed,
# diffed, or converted as text.
examples/inspiration/** -text
examples/inspiration/water-lilies/brand/assets/inspiration.jpg binary

# ...but PROVENANCE.md is hand-authored and human-edited, and has no producer
# counterpart at all - it is NOT part of the byte-identity comparison the rule
# above exists for. Keep git's newline normalization on it (this repo has
# core.autocrlf enabled) so a later edit from a CRLF-emitting editor cannot
# commit mixed line endings. More specific, and listed LAST so it wins over
# the tree-wide -text; eol=lf keeps the checkout matching the rest of the tree.
examples/inspiration/**/PROVENANCE.md text eol=lf
